<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Morton Stanley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Morton Stanley was a Welsh-American journalist and explorer who found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> in 1871 and crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1874–1877, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba</a> and Congo to the Atlantic with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a>. From 1879 he worked for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> of the Belgians, gathering treaties with Congolese chiefs that became the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley.jpg" alt="Henry Morton Stanley" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Creator:J. Russel &amp; Sons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:No-nb_bldsa_1c051_Henry_Morton_Stanley.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Henry Morton Stanley</b> completed his descent of the Congo River in 1877, Leopold hired him, and from 1879 to 1884 Stanley built stations along the river, including Vivi and Léopoldville, and gathered hundreds of treaties by which Congolese chiefs ceded land and rights to Leopold’s organisations, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its open court made it the gateway through which Islam and Christianity entered the interior of East Africa: Mutesa I received Arab traders and, after the journalist <b>Henry Morton Stanley</b> publicised his interest in 1875, Protestant and Catholic missionaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Zanzibar was also the starting point for European exploration of the interior: Richard Burton, John Hanning Speke, David Livingstone and <b>Henry Morton Stanley</b> all outfitted their expeditions in the town, often with the help of its merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The journalist <b>Henry Morton Stanley</b> located the missionary-explorer David Livingstone at Ujiji, on Lake Tanganyika, by Stanley's own dating.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Morton Stanley</b>, who met him in 1876, compared him to Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/state/mirambos-state">Mirambo's state</a></li><li><blockquote>Tippu Tip helped <b>Henry Morton Stanley</b> down the Lualaba in 1876, and in 1887 the Congo Free State appointed him governor of the Stanley Falls district.</blockquote><a href="/state/tippu-tips-state">Tippu Tip's State</a></li></ul></section>
